{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tm1hzYjRnKLL6kMtVAvzJQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Q3 platform review — engineering all-hands notes\n2\t\n3\tLast quarter our API p95 latency drifted from 180 ms to 340 ms. The cause was\n4\tnot the code we merged — the two features that shipped were both well-tested\n5\tand behind flags. It was the flags themselves. The evaluator we chose eighteen\n6\tmonths ago fetches its rules from a config service on every request, and we\n7\thad crossed a threshold where that config service began to throttle us during\n8\tthe daily 09:00 traffic spike.\n9\t\n10\tThe team spent six weeks arguing whether to replace the evaluator (a big\n11\tmigration, no shipping features for a quarter) or to cache its results (small\n12\tchange, unclear correctness implications when flags update). We picked the\n13\tcache. We wrote it in a week, wrote the invalidation contract in another\n14\tweek, and shipped it behind its own flag. p95 is back to 190 ms and holding.\n15\t\n16\tTwo things came out of this that generalize. First: instrument the machinery\n17\tyou did not write. We had beautiful graphs of our own service and none of\n18\tthe config service that was actually slow — the outage was invisible for\n19\tthree days on our dashboards. Second: when a debate stalls, run the small\n20\texperiment. The cache took less time to build than the argument about whether\n21\tto build it took to have.\n22\t\n23\tNext quarter we will audit every third-party client we depend on for the same\n24\tpattern — hidden per-request work behind an API that looks free.\n25\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"33333333-3333-3333-3333-333333333333","uuid":"ef89ccf5-1d7b-405f-8dd5-7c16b7327c99","timestamp":"2026-09-10T09:47:37.672Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/cc-claude-skills-scratch/article.md","content":"# Q3 platform review — engineering all-hands notes\n\nLast quarter our API p95 latency drifted from 180 ms to 340 ms. The cause was\nnot the code we merged — the two features that shipped were both well-tested\nand behind flags. It was the flags themselves. The evaluator we chose eighteen\nmonths ago fetches its rules from a config service on every request, and we\nhad crossed a threshold where that config service began to throttle us during\nthe daily 09:00 traffic spike.\n\nThe team spent six weeks arguing whether to replace the evaluator (a big\nmigration, no shipping features for a quarter) or to cache its results (small\nchange, unclear correctness implications when flags update). We picked the\ncache. We wrote it in a week, wrote the invalidation contract in another\nweek, and shipped it behind its own flag. p95 is back to 190 ms and holding.\n\nTwo things came out of this that generalize. First: instrument the machinery\nyou did not write. We had beautiful graphs of our own service and none of\nthe config service that was actually slow — the outage was invisible for\nthree days on our dashboards. Second: when a debate stalls, run the small\nexperiment. The cache took less time to build than the argument about whether\nto build it took to have.\n\nNext quarter we will audit every third-party client we depend on for the same\npattern — hidden per-request work behind an API that looks free.\n","numLines":25,"startLine":1,"totalLines":25}}}
